## What happened

Apple's security frameworks dropped DSA support, and .NET followed suit. Starting in .NET 11, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS. Linux (OpenSSL) and Windows (CNG) still support it, which is why only macOS breaks.

## How to fix it

You have two options:

### Option 1 — Use BouncyCastle (recommended if you must keep verifying legacy DSA signatures)

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

Replace the built-in DSA call with BouncyCastle's managed implementation, which works on all platforms:

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] message, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

### Option 2 — Migrate to ECDSA (recommended long-term)

If you control both signing and verification, migrate away from legacy DSA entirely:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
byte[] sig = ecdsa.SignData(data, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
bool ok = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, sig, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

This is supported on all platforms and is the modern replacement.

---

**TL;DR:** macOS no longer provides a native DSA implementation, and .NET 11 removed the fallback. Use BouncyCastle for a cross-platform managed DSA, or migrate to ECDSA/EdDSA if possible.
